WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.610
Look at a political map of Africa.

2
00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:05.630
Many of its borders run
straight as a ruler.

3
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.010
Even a border fixed on
paper can stay unmarked.

4
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:13.830
In 2002, a commission fixed
the Eritrea–Ethiopia border.

5
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.250
Marking it on the ground stalled.

6
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:20.510
So why are so many of Africa's
borders ruler-straight lines?

7
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:21.810
Flat deserts?

8
00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:22.810
Straight rivers?

9
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.710
A choice to avoid wars?

10
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.010
In this mission, you'll
follow how a border is made:

11
00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:31.930
defined, delimited,
demarcated and administered.

12
00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.830
Then you'll sort borders into six types,
such as a language divide in Belgium,

13
00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.550
and meet a ceasefire line
that still divides Korea.

14
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:45.710
Your mission: explain why so many of
Africa's borders are ruler-straight lines.
